4.1 Making an extract
=====================

Select the passage and press ‘C-c r e’.  That works in pdf-tools,
pdf-canvas, nov.el, eww, Org, Markdown and text, and inside an extract
(which makes a *sub-extract*).  In a PDF, the passage is also
highlighted and saved into the file.  ‘C-u C-c r e’ asks for a priority.

   Several passages at once: in a PDF, drag with ‘C-’ held for each;
with the multi-region package, any selection it holds.  They become one
extract (‘C-u C-u C-c r e’: one each).


File: readq.info,  Node: Highlight keep or review,  Next: Reviewing extracts,  Prev: Making an extract,  Up: Extracts

4.2 Highlight, keep or review
=============================

Not every passage deserves a place in the queue.  Select it, then:

‘C-c r H’   *highlight* it and nothing more
‘C-c r E’   make an extract that is *kept* as a note but never comes up
‘C-c r e’   make an extract that comes back for *review*

   A highlight in a PDF is saved into the PDF, in green; in an EPUB, a
text or a web page, readq remembers it and shows it when you read there.
‘C-c r J’ lists the highlights of a book and jumps to one; ‘C-u C-c r H’
on a highlight removes it.

   You can change your mind later.  On a highlight, with nothing
selected, ‘C-c r e’ or ‘C-c r E’ makes it an extract.  On a kept
extract, ‘C-c r F’ puts it in the queue.


File: readq.info,  Node: Reviewing extracts,  Next: Editing an extract beside the book,  Prev: Highlight keep or review,  Up: Extracts

4.3 Reviewing extracts
======================

When ‘C-c r n’ brings up an extract, it opens its note.  Then:

   • *Shorten it.*  Delete what you don't need and rewrite the rest.
     Each review boils it down a little.  This is the heart of
     incremental reading.
   • ‘C-c r e’ on part of it makes a sub-extract.
   • ‘C-c r g’ shows the passage in the book.  Your bookmark stays where
     it was; ‘C-c r o’ goes back to it.
   • ‘C-c r '’ edits the extract in a window beside the book.
   • ‘C-c r d’ dismisses it: out of the queue, note kept.
   • ‘C-c r c’ marks it ready for a flashcard (see *note Flashcards::).
   • ‘C-c r n’ moves on.


File: readq.info,  Node: Editing an extract beside the book,  Next: Where extracts are stored,  Prev: Reviewing extracts,  Up: Extracts

4.4 Editing an extract beside the book
======================================

While reading, ‘C-c r '’ opens an extract's note in a window beside the
book.  Which one:

   • in an EPUB, a text or a web page, the extract highlighted where the
     cursor is;
   • in a PDF, the one on the page shown, or one you choose when there
     are several.  Quicker: *click its highlight* and its note opens.
     Clicking other highlights does what pdf-tools does.  To turn this
     off, set ‘readq-click-opens-extract’ to nil.

   Edits go straight into the note.  ‘C-c C-c’ saves it and closes the
window; if you changed the title, the file is renamed.  Editing doesn't
count as a review or change when the extract comes back.

   Denote's commands work in that window: ‘C-c n k’ to change keywords,
‘C-c n l’ to insert a link, and so on.  They act on the extract's note.
This holds for commands called with a key or from a menu, not with
‘M-x’.  Keywords you add are kept; readq's own (‘extract’ and the book's
tags) come back if you remove them.  Other commands can be added through
‘readq-edit-extract-note-commands’.

   The window is on the right, 40% of the frame wide: see
‘readq-edit-extract-side’ and ‘readq-edit-extract-size’.


File: readq.info,  Node: Where extracts are stored,  Next: Figures,  Prev: Editing an extract beside the book,  Up: Extracts

4.5 Where extracts are stored
=============================

Each extract is one Org file in ‘~/notes/’, named the way Denote
(https://protesilaos.com/emacs/denote) names notes, so Denote,
consult-notes or plain Dired all work with them:

     20261115T142233==guyton=physiology=p0057--systole-is-the-phase__cardio_extract.org

     #+title:      Systole is the phase of contraction.
     #+filetags:   :cardio:extract:
     #+identifier: 20261115T142233
     #+signature:  guyton=physiology=p0057
     #+source:     [[readq:20261115T142233][Guyton Physiology, p. 57]]

     #+begin_quote
     Systole is the phase of contraction.
     #+end_quote

   The title can be anything; edit it and the file is renamed.  ‘C-c r
Z’ lists a book's notes in Dired, in book order.  To keep them
elsewhere, set ‘readq-extracts-directory’.

* Menu:

* Silos::


File: readq.info,  Node: Silos,  Up: Where extracts are stored

4.5.1 Silos
-----------

A book can keep its extracts in a Denote silo
(https://protesilaos.com/emacs/denote) of its own, a separate notes
folder, so that pathology extracts go to ‘~/Pathology/notes/’ and the
rest to ‘~/notes/’.  The silos are those of ‘denote-silo-directories’,
or of ‘readq-silos’ if you set it.

   A book you add gets the silo whose parent folder holds the book's
file: with ‘~/Pathology/notes/’ a silo, a book in ‘~/Pathology/Breast/’
keeps its extracts there.  ‘C-c r @’ (‘readq-set-silo’), or ‘@’ on the
dashboard, chooses another one; the notes already made move with it.
All books stay in the one queue.

   Coming from readq 0.1?  ‘M-x readq-convert-extracts’ turns your old
extracts into notes like these.  The old files are left alone.


File: readq.info,  Node: Figures,  Next: Finding an extract,  Prev: Where extracts are stored,  Up: Extracts

4.6 Figures
===========

‘C-c r P’ makes a diagram or picture an extract.  In a PDF, select the
area with ‘M-drag’ first; in an EPUB, web page or Org file, put point on
the image; in a comic, it takes the page.  Pictures copied elsewhere
(SumatraPDF, a browser) are taken from the clipboard with ‘C-c r y’.

   Figures are saved in ‘~/notes/figures/’ and shown in the extract.
They go to Anki and org-srs with the card.


File: readq.info,  Node: Finding an extract,  Next: Cleaning up,  Prev: Figures,  Up: Extracts

4.7 Finding an extract
======================

Key         What it does
--------------------------------------------------------------------
‘C-c r S’   search the text of all extracts as you type (consult)
‘C-c r j’   pick an extract from a list, by book, text or tag
‘C-c r Z’   a book's extracts in Dired


File: readq.info,  Node: Cleaning up,  Next: Highlights from other PDF viewers,  Prev: Finding an extract,  Up: Extracts

4.8 Cleaning up
===============

Extracts you keep postponing pile up.  ‘C-c r X’ walks through the
*stale* ones, reviewed often or long ago without becoming anything, and
asks for each: make it a card, merge it into another, dismiss, delete,
or keep.

   ‘C-c r D’ deletes an extract with its note and its highlight in the
PDF.  ‘C-c r d’ only takes it out of the queue.


File: readq.info,  Node: Highlights from other PDF viewers,  Prev: Cleaning up,  Up: Extracts

4.9 Highlights from other PDF viewers
=====================================

Highlights saved into a PDF by SumatraPDF, Okular or a tablet app become
extracts: ‘C-c r i’ for the book at point, ‘C-c r I’ for all PDFs.
readq also looks for new ones before suggesting what to read.  Needs
pdf-tools.


File: readq.info,  Node: Flashcards,  Next: Your data,  Prev: Extracts,  Up: Top

5 Flashcards
************

Once an extract holds just one fact, make it a card:

  1. *Shape it.*  For a cloze, select the words to hide and press ‘C-c r
     k’ (‘C-u’ adds a hint).  Otherwise the extract's *title is the
     question* and its text the answer: rewrite the title as a question.
  2. *Mark it ready* with ‘C-c r c’.  It leaves the reading queue.
  3. *Export* all ready extracts with ‘C-c r C’.

   readq asks where to send them, or set it once:

     (setq readq-flashcard-backend 'anki)   ; or 'org-srs

   • *Anki:* keep Anki open with the AnkiConnect add-on (code
     ‘2055492159’).  Cards go into the deck ‘readq::<book>’.  If you
     edit an extract later, ‘C-c r U’ updates its card and keeps its
     reviews.  Without AnkiConnect, readq writes a file for _File >
     Import_.
   • *org-srs:* cards go into ‘<book>-cards.org’ next to your notes.
     Review them with ‘M-x org-srs-review-start’.

