File: readq.info,  Node: Drafts from a local model,  Next: Cards you keep failing,  Up: Flashcards

5.1 Drafts from a local model
=============================

If you use gptel, ‘C-c r Q’ on an extract asks its model for three card
drafts, clozes and questions, from the extract's text.  Choose one: it
is written into the note for you to correct, then ‘C-c r c’ as usual.
The text goes only to the model gptel is set up with, so with a local
model nothing leaves your computer.  Drafts can be wrong: check them
against the passage.  ‘readq-draft-count’ and ‘readq-draft-instructions’
change what is asked.


File: readq.info,  Node: Cards you keep failing,  Prev: Drafts from a local model,  Up: Flashcards

5.2 Cards you keep failing
==========================

A card failed three times was learned without its context.  Its extract
then comes back into the reading queue, due today, and opens with the
passage in the book beside it: reread, rework the extract, ‘C-c r n’.
With org-srs, readq counts each "again".  With Anki, it asks once a day
while Anki is running (‘M-x readq-check-failed-cards’ asks now).
‘readq-card-lapse-threshold’ sets the number; ‘nil’ turns it off.


File: readq.info,  Node: Your data,  Next: Customizing,  Prev: Flashcards,  Up: Top

6 Your data
***********

Everything readq keeps is in ‘~/notes/’:

File               Holds
-----------------------------------------------------------------------
‘readq.eld’        the queue: priorities, dates, places, history
‘readq-backups/’   a copy of the queue from each of the last 14 days
‘*__extract.org’   your extracts, one note each
‘figures/’         the pictures of figure extracts

   Back up the whole folder.  If the queue file is ever damaged, ‘C-c r
B’ puts back a daily copy.  The queue file is plain text, so it syncs
well, but don't run readq on two computers at the same time.

   If you do, readq notices.  When another program, such as a second
Emacs or a sync program, changed the queue file since readq read it,
readq does not save over it: it says so once, and keeps what you do in
this Emacs without saving it.  Then choose which queue to keep:

   • ‘M-x readq-reload-database’ reads the file as it is now.  What you
     did in this Emacs since is dropped.
   • ‘M-x readq-save-database’ saves this Emacs's queue over the file,
     after asking.  The other program's changes are dropped.

   If you close Emacs before choosing, it asks you.


File: readq.info,  Node: Customizing,  Next: When something goes wrong,  Prev: Your data,  Up: Top

7 Customizing
*************

‘M-x customize-group RET readq’ lists every option with its
documentation.  The ones people change most:

Option                          Default      What it sets
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
‘readq-daily-minutes’           90           the daily budget; nil for none
‘readq-default-priority’        50           the priority offered when adding
‘readq-book-max-interval’       7            days between sessions of a priority-100 book
‘readq-extracts-directory’      ‘~/notes/’   where extracts are stored
‘readq-silos’                   nil          other folders a book's extracts can go to
‘readq-default-pdf-viewer’      ‘emacs’      ‘sumatra’ to read PDFs in SumatraPDF
‘readq-flashcard-backend’       ‘ask’        ‘anki’ or ‘org-srs’
‘readq-extract-add-highlight’   t            highlight extracts in the PDF
‘readq-dashboard-columns’                    which columns the dashboard shows

   ‘C-h v’ on any option says more.  For code that extends readq, ‘C-h f
readq-define-viewer’ explains how to support another document viewer,
and the hooks are listed by ‘M-x apropos-variable RET
^readq-.*-\(hook\|functions\)’.

   Two functions are there for other packages that want to add to the
queue.  ‘(readq-current-book)’ returns the book of the queue shown in
the current buffer, or nil.  ‘(readq-add-extract TEXT :book BOOK :page
PAGE :title TITLE :tags TAGS)’ adds TEXT as an extract of BOOK (the book
shown in the current buffer when BOOK is left out) and returns it; with
‘:keep t’ it is kept as a note, out of the queue, as with ‘C-c r E’.
my-gptel-study uses them for ‘C-c C-e’ in an answer buffer.  ‘C-h f’ on
either says more.


